<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaghjagh River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mygdonius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jaghjagh River is a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> that rises in the Tur Abdin hills of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and flows south past Nusaybin and Qamishli into north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Known in antiquity as the Mygdonius, it watered one of the most densely settled parts of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, with sites such as Tell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brak">Brak</a> and Tell Barri, ancient Kahat, along its course.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaghjagh-river.jpg" alt="Jaghjagh River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ftkurt · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%87a%C4%9F_%C3%87a%C4%9F_deresi,_Nusaybin,_Musa_Anter_Park%C4%B1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
